$DIR/main.p:24:2: cannot call q.Drop(): q is a *Plain, which only points at a Plain that something else owns
    Drop releases what an owner holds. The Plain q points to belongs to another
    variable or place, which will drop it when its life ends, so dropping it
    here would release it twice.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    To end an owner's life early, call Drop on the variable that holds it; it
    cannot be used afterwards:
        v.Drop() // v is the Plain variable itself
    To leave an empty value behind instead:
        old := take(q)
    (see "Drops" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.6)
    (run "pluto explain E3307" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:25:7: cannot use p.Drop as a value: Drop can only be called on the owner itself
    A method value could be called at any time, by anyone, and each call would
    release what the owner holds.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Call p.Drop() where the owner's life should end.
    (see "Drops" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.6)
    (run "pluto explain E3307" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:29:2: cannot call h.log.Drop(): h.log is part of another value, which drops it when it is dropped
    Drop releases what an owner holds. h.log belongs to the value it is part of,
    which will drop it again when its own life ends.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    To release it now and leave an empty value in its place:
        old := take(&h.log)
        old.Drop()
    (see "Drops" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.6)
    (run "pluto explain E3307" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:33:12: cannot move *pl out of the value pl points to
    Log is an owner type, so using *pl here would move it. But the value belongs
    to whatever pl points into, which would free it again when it is dropped.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    To move it out and leave an empty value in its place:
        v := take(pl)
    To look at it without moving it:
        v := pl
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3303" for a longer explanation)
